#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# function_continuous

#% text
# `function_continuous(f, x)` gives `true` when `f` is continuous for every real `x`, and `false` when it is not; `function_continuous([f, cond], x)` asks where `cond` holds, and `complexes` asks over the complex numbers.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The answer decides whether the discontinuities of `function_discontinuities` meet the region: by `reduce` for one real variable, by the cylindrical decomposition for polynomial conditions in several variables, over the real and imaginary parts for complex variables, and otherwise by a numeric witness (a sign change refined by bisection).

#% item
# With parameters the answer is `conditional_expression(true, c)`: `c` is the condition on the parameters under which the discontinuities miss the region (a sufficient condition when the discontinuities are not polynomial).

#% item
# `generate_conditions: false` gives the value without its condition; `automatic` leaves out the conditions `p != q`; `none` leaves the call unevaluated when a condition would be needed.

#% item
# `assumptions:` (or `assuming`) gives facts about the parameters.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
function_continuous(sin(x), x)
#% output
#= true

#% cell
function_continuous(floor(x), x)
#% output
#= false

#% text
# The logarithm is continuous on its domain:

#% cell
function_continuous([log(x), x > 0], x)
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Over the complexes the square root has a branch cut:

#% cell
function_continuous(sqrt(x), x, complexes)
#% output
#= false

#% text
# A pole on the unit circle, and none:

#% cell
[function_continuous(erf(sin(x + y)/(x^2 + y^2 - 1)), [x, y]), function_continuous(erf(sin(x + y)/(x^2 + y^2 + 1)), [x, y])]
#% output
#= [false, true]

#% text
# Over the complexes `x^2 + y^2 + 1` has zeros:

#% cell
function_continuous(erf(sin(x + y)/(x^2 + y^2 + 1)), [x, y], complexes)
#% output
#= false

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# A parameter gives a conditional answer:

#% cell
function_continuous(log(x^2 + a), x)
#% output
#= conditional_expression(true, a > 0)

#% cell
function_continuous(cos(x*y)/a, [x, y], generate_conditions: automatic)
#% output
#= conditional_expression(true, element(a, reals))

#% cell
function_continuous(sin(x + y)/(exp(x*y) + a), [x, y], assumptions: a > 0)
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Continuous on the reals, not on the complexes (poles at `2 im` and `-2 im`):

#% cell
[function_continuous(4/(x^2 + 4), x), function_continuous(4/(x^2 + 4), x, complexes)]
#% output
#= [true, false]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A piecewise function is analysed through its pieces and the points where they meet: here the pieces differ by 1 at 0, and `unit_step` jumps there too:

#% cell
[function_continuous(piecewise([[x, x < 0], [x + 1, x >= 0]], 0), x), function_continuous(unit_step(x), x)]
#% output
#= [false, false]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `function_discontinuities`, `function_analytic`, `function_meromorphic`, `function_domain`
